      "type": "answer",
      "tags": [
        "table"
      ],
      "question": "In the circular economy table, what does formally recognising MRFs achieve?",
      "answer": "Formally recognising MRFs strengthens the middle of the chain [3]. It formalises MRFs as sorting facilities and allows them to act as deposition points for multiple waste streams, including special care and sanitary waste, for further processing [3]. This recognition increases the probability that recyclables retain market-acceptable quality [2]. Key enabling mechanisms include reduced organic contamination of dry recyclables, improved sorting efficiency and residue control at MRFs, and formal integration of authorised recyclers and aggregators [2]. From a circular economy lens, the Rules improve material value retention, not merely diversion rates [2]. Clean feedstock enables higher-order recycling pathways, reduces down-cycling, and stabilises secondary material markets—an essential condition for private-sector participation [2].",
